  "conclusion": "Artifact integrity passes. Recovery512 has large consistent elapsed, CPU and allocation improvements; recovery0 improves in both pairs. Candidate-only durable normal elapsed tail spikes recur in both cohorts (7/60 above50ms, 5/60 above90ms, versus0/60 base). Median improvements do not settle this potential material tail regression; causality needs host/wakeup investigation and/or a fresh matched comparison.",
  "caveats": [
    "One hosted job with two paired cohorts: descriptive differences, not an independently calibrated timing gate.",
    "Only recovery0/recovery512 and minimal/durable controls were run; this is not renewed full-matrix coverage.",
    "Criterion headline timing is separate from normal CPU/elapsed diagnostics and allocator-instrumented diagnostics; instrumented elapsed is not used for timing claims.",
    "Requested Rust allocation bytes/counts exclude direct C/SQLite allocation and are not peak RSS or retained memory.",
    "Recovery includes SQLite open, claim and completion; template construction, database copying and store close are excluded.",
    "No per-operation latency array or first-output samples are populated for these four cases; normal elapsed samples are single-run completion measurements.",
    "Minimal CPU diagnostics show large first-base/cohort variation despite unchanged minimal allocation counts; do not claim that diagnostic CPU change as an optimization result.",
    "Durable normal timing has candidate-only tail spikes in both cohorts: 2/30 and5/30 above50ms, with unchanged useful work/database counters and CPU about6.4\u20138.1ms for all >30ms spikes. Base stays below15.1ms; allocator-instrumented captures show no >30ms spikes. This is an unresolved tail concern despite improved Criterion medians.",
    "Executable hashes are recorded and stable between cohorts; executables themselves are not included in the artifact, so their bytes cannot be independently rehashed here."
  ],
